"EBT-Policy: Energy Unlocks Emergent Physical Reasoning Capabilities (https://energy-based-transformers.github.io/ebt-policy/)\nciteturn2155view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://energy-based-transformers.github.io/ebt-policy/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 95\nL0: # ⚡️ EBT-Policy: Energy Unlocks Emergent Physical Reasoning Capabilities\nL1: \nL2: cite0†Travis Davies†github.com ^{1}, cite1†Yiqi Huang†github.com ^{1}, cite2†Alexi Gladstone†alexiglad.github.io ^{2}, Yunxin Liu^{3}, Xiang Chen^{4}, cite3†Heng Ji†blender.cs.illinois.edu ^{2}, cite4†Huxian Liu†github.com ^{1}, cite5†Luhui Hu†scholar.google.com ^{1}\nL3: \nL4: ^{1}ZhiCheng AI    ^{2}UIUC    ^{3}Tsinghua University    ^{4}Peking University\nL5: \nL6: cite6†Paper cite7†arXiv†arxiv.org cite8†Tweet1†x.com cite9†Tweet2†x.com cite10†Code (Coming Soon) L7: ## TL;DR\nL8: \nL9: Implicit policies parameterized by generative models, such as Diffusion Policy, have become the standard for policy learning and Vision–Language–Action (VLA) models in robotics. However, these approaches often suffer from high computational cost, exposure bias, and unstable inference dynamics, which lead to divergence under distribution shifts.\nL10: We introduce EBT-Policy, a new energy-based architecture that solves core issues in robotic and real-world settings. EBT-Policy consistently outperforms diffusion-based policies across simulated and real-world tasks, while requiring significantly less training and inference computation.\nL11: \nL12: * * *\nL13:   * ⚡️ 50× faster inference — converges in just 2 steps vs. Diffusion Policy's 100\nL14:   * ⚡️ 66% fewer training epochs — faster convergence during training\nL15:   * ⚡️ Emergent zero-shot retry behavior — recovers from failures using only behavior cloning data, without explicit retry training\nL16:   * ⚡️ Uncertainty-aware inference — leverages scalar energy for dynamic compute allocation\nL17: \nL18: cite11†Image: Energy landscape minimization and uncertainty modeling diagram L19: Explaining Uncertainty Modeling. Twelve frames are grouped into three phases: (1) Tool Insertion, (2) Hook Hanging Attempt, and (3) Recovery & Successful Retry. The color bar beneath each frame encodes per-frame energy predicted by the model, where a lower energy indicates higher certainty in EBT-Policy. Notably, red (Step 7) marks the failure that triggers an EBT-Policy retry, while green (Step 11) marks the successful correction.\nL20: Together, these steps highlight EBT-Policy's interpretability and physical reasoning: using energy-based uncertainty to decide whether to continue or retry and how to adjust actions.\nL21: Explaining Energy Minimization. EBT-Policy receives inputs (RGB frames, robotic proprioception, and language instructions) and assigns an energy to candidate action trajectories. Starting from a noisy initialization, the trajectory is iteratively updated by gradient descent on this energy, yielding starting states to a final executable plan. Optimization terminates when the energy converges to a minimum, as illustrated by the energy-landscape sketch.\nL22: ## Abstract\nL23: \nL24: ## Demonstrations\nL25: \nL26: ### RoboMimic Benchmark\nL27: \nL28: Your browser does not support the video tag.\nL29: \nL30: Can\nL31: Robotic can manipulation task demonstrating robust performance.\nL32: \nL33: Your browser does not support the video tag.\nL34: \nL35: Lift\nL36: Object lifting task showcasing energy-based policy learning.\nL37: \nL38: Your browser does not support the video tag.\nL39: \nL40: Square\nL41: Square nut assembly task with precise manipulation.\nL42: ### Emergent Retry Behavior\nL43: \nL44: Your browser does not support the video tag.\nL45: \nL46: Tool Hang\nL47: Demonstrates emergent zero-shot retry behavior without explicit retry training.\nL48: ### Real-World Tasks\nL49: \nL50: Your browser does not support the video tag.\nL51: \nL52: Your browser does not support the video tag.\nL53: \nL54: Fold Towel (3 inference steps)\nL55: Real-world robotic towel folding with robust generalization and consistent performance across variations.\nL56: \nL57: Your browser does not support the video tag.\nL58: \nL59: Your browser does not support the video tag.\nL60: \nL61: Pick and Place (3 inference steps)\nL62: Demonstrates emergent behavior with robust manipulation and uncertainty-aware inference.\nL63: Your browser does not support the video tag.\nL64: \nL65: Your browser does not support the video tag.\nL66: \nL67: Place two Plates (3 inference steps)\nL68: Precise placement task showcasing energy-based reasoning and energy landscape optimization for accurate placement.\nL69: ## Results\nL70: \nL71: cite12†Image: Success Rates During Training L72: \nL73: Success Rates During Training. EBT-Policy exhibits rapid performance improvement, reaching 100% success by epoch 30, using just 2 iterations for predicting actions. Diffusion Policy (DP), on the other hand, only reaches a 100% success rate after 90 epochs, and uses 50 times more steps than EBT-Policy at inference, demonstrating how EBT-Policy is more efficient than DP during both training and inference.\nL74: Property  | EBT-Policy-S  | EBT-Policy-R\nL75: --- | --- | ---\nL76: Size  | ~30M  | ~100M\nL77: Task  | Simulation  | Real World\nL78: Language Encoder  | N/A  | T5-S\nL79: Vision Encoder  | ResNet-18  | DINOv3-S\nL80: \nL81: Comparison of EBT-Policy variants. EBT-Policy-S is a compact Transformer used for controlled simulation studies, while EBT-Policy-R is a larger multimodal variant designed for real-world, language-conditioned, and multitask policy learning.\nL82: ## BibTeX [Button: Copy]\nL83: \nL84:     `@misc{davies2025ebtpolicyenergyunlocksemergent,\nL85:           title={EBT-Policy: Energy Unlocks Emergent Physical Reasoning Capabilities},\nL86:           author={Travis Davies and Yiqi Huang and Alexi Gladstone and Yunxin Liu and Xiang Chen and Heng Ji and Huxian Liu and Luhui Hu},\nL87:           year={2025},\nL88:           eprint={2510.27545},\nL89:           archivePrefix={arXiv},\nL90:           primaryClass={cs.RO},\nL91:           url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.27545},\nL92:     }`\nL93: This page was built using the cite13†Academic Project Page Template†github.com which was adopted from the cite14†Nerfies†nerfies.github.io project page. You are free to borrow the source code of this website, we just ask that you link back to this page in the footer.\nL94: This website is licensed under a cite15†Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License†creativecommons.org .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInteract-RAG: Reason and Interact with the Corpus, Beyond Black-Box Retrieval (https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2026/hash/b61f288da3c106f65d57b0d45b470b6b-Abstract-Conference.html)\nciteturn2155view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2026/hash/b61f288da3c106f65d57b0d45b470b6b-Abstract-Conference.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 20\nL0: # Interact-RAG: Reason and Interact with the Corpus, Beyond Black-Box Retrieval\nL1: \nL2: Yulong Hui, Chao Chen, Zhihang Fu, Yihao Liu, Jieping Ye, Huanchen Zhang\nL3: \nL4: cite0†International Conference on Learning Representations 2026 (ICLR 2026) Conference\nL5: \nL6: cite1†Bibtex cite2†Paper cite3†Supplemental L7: ## Abstract\nL8: Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has significantly enhanced LLMs by incorporating external information. However, prevailing agentic RAG approaches are constrained by a critical limitation: they treat the retrieval process as a black-box querying operation. This confines agents' actions to query issuing, hindering its ability to tackle complex information-seeking tasks.\nL9: To address this, we introduce Interact-RAG, a new paradigm that elevates the LLM agent from a passive query issuer into an active manipulator of the retrieval process. We dismantle the black-box with a Corpus Interaction Engine, equipping the agent with a set of action primitives for fine-grained control over information retrieval.\nL10: To further empower the agent on the entire RAG pipeline, we first develop a reasoning-enhanced workflow, which enables both zero-shot execution and the synthesis of interaction trajectories. We then leverage this synthetic data to train a fully autonomous end-to-end agent via Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), followed by refinement with Reinforcement Learning (RL).\nL11: Extensive experiments across six benchmarks demonstrate that Interact-RAG significantly outperforms other advanced methods, validating the efficacy of our reasoning-interaction strategy.\nL12: #### Name Change Policy\nL13: \nL14: Requests for name changes in the electronic proceedings will be accepted with no questions asked. However name changes may cause bibliographic tracking issues. Authors are asked to consider this carefully and discuss it with their co-authors prior to requesting a name change in the electronic proceedings.\nL15: \nL16: Use the \"Report an Issue\" link to request a name change.\nL17: \nL18: cite4†Report an Issue†iclr.cc |    cite5†Name Change Policy L19: Do not remove: This comment is monitored to verify that the site is working properly\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - thu-ml/TetraJet-v2-NVFP4Training: [ICML 2026 Spotlight] Official implementation of TetraJet-v2: Accurate NVFP4 Training for LLMs, with fully-NVFP4 linear layer with unbiased backprop, and algorithms to overcome LLMs' weight-oscillation and activation-outlier bottlenecks. · GitHub (https://github.com/thu-ml/TetraJet-v2-NVFP4Training)\nciteturn2155view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/thu-ml/TetraJet-